# SELinux rules for the SfAnalysis injection module (M8).
#
# KernelSU-Next loads `<module>/sepolicy.rule` at boot (same mechanism the
# rezygisk / zygisk_vector modules use).
#
# Why these two:
#
#   execmem  — libsfanalysis_rs.so builds its inline-hook trampolines on an
#              anonymous mmap(PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE|PROT_EXEC) page. Without this
#              rule the mmap fails EACCES and no hook is ever installed
#              (device-verified: `NOT hooked ioctl: mmap errno=13` x4, and
#              `avc denied { execmem } ... tclass=process` on an unpatched boot).
#
#   execmod  — installing a hook also mprotects the target's file-backed code
#              page (libc.so, context system_lib_file) to RWX to write the
#              16-byte jump. That is the `execmod` check.
#
# The vendor's own sfanalysis / ssanalysis modules ship no sepolicy.rule at all,
# so upstream never solved this; the feature is simply dead on enforcing builds.

allow surfaceflinger surfaceflinger process execmem
allow surfaceflinger system_lib_file file execmod
